The payment page stops asking the client to ring before paying

"don't need to telephone the office before sending." (JJ, 2026-10-09)
The warning opened by telling the client to telephone the office and
confirm the details with someone they had already spoken to, before
sending any money. That instruction is gone.

What stays is the sentence that actually defeats the attack: the firm
never emails changed payment instructions, so an email that does is a
fake, whoever it appears to come from. A number to ring is still printed
for the client who receives one. The warning still comes before the
account details on the page, and is still a border rather than a
background, so it survives a print with Background graphics unticked.

The new opening line went in with an em dash and check-retainer.js
rejected it, which is the firm's own convention doing its job.

